            <h2 class="backlink"><a href="../credits/index.html">Credits</a></h2>
            <h3>Moon Icon</h2>
                <p class="sub-header">Hand-created in Inkscape by AECochran</p>
                <p>Converted to ico format using
                    <a href="https://picflow.com/convert/svg-to-ico" target="_blank"
                        rel="noopener noreferrer">Picflow.com</a>
                </p>
                <img src="../assets/key-branding/Moonogram-icon-final.svg" alt="Moonogram">
                <p class="see also"> See <a href="../projects/favicon.html">the Favicon project page for additional
                        information.

                        <p>This started as an attempt to turn the monogram I initially created into a profile image I
                            could use
                            in places that require round images.</p>
                        <p>When I first uploaded it, I noticed that the crescent made by the
                            circled C stood out the most. Because of that, I tried applying a bright yellow, making the
                            background a dark blue, adding a circle gradient to imitate a glow, then covering the glow
                            up for
                            the shadowed
                            side of the moon, then I tried changing the color of the circles and A. Eventually I had
                            something I
                            liked.</p>
                        <p>From there, I tried converting it to ico with the first converter I found, <a
                                href="https://cloudconvert.com/svg-to-ico" target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer">CloudConvert.com</a>. When I uploaded that ico to my file and
                            checked
                            my web browser, though, the ico had white where I wanted transparent, so I tried another, <a
                                href="https://picflow.com/convert/svg-to-ico" target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer">Picflow.com</a>. </p>
                        <p>This time, the transparency worked. Things seemed good enough. Until I published my site
                            accessed it
                            from my iPhone, and bookmarked the page and got an A on a dark icon instead of my favicon.
                        </p>
                        <p>I though that couldn't be right. So I went searching again, and I found <a
                                href="https://realfavicongenerator.net/" target="_blank"
                                rel="noopener noreferrer">RealFaviconGenerator.net</a>, and I tried one more time.</p>
                        <p>And, well... It was weird when I gave it my SVG. It took only the top corner instead of
                            shrinking it
                            down. So I exported my SVG. And it created a new svg and just created a bunch of png files
                            plus a
                            webmanifest file. I hadn't heard of web manifests yet.</p>
                        <p>Since I already had an svg, I just replaced all of the files other than the manifest with my
                            own exports and maintained the better-quality ico Picflow created despite the large size
                            difference.</p>
